HST Office reads administrator policies and registry settings from its own Windows keys, not LibreOffice's.

configmgr read its two Windows policy layers from HKLM and HKCU SOFTWARE\Policies\LibreOffice, so every
policy an administrator set for LibreOffice also applied to HST Office, and the other way round.

- configmgr: the policy layers read SOFTWARE\Policies\HASON TECH\HST Office (the vendor and product
  names of the MSI's own HKLM\Software\HASON TECH\HST Office key).
- Transition: releases up to 1.0.0-beta.4 read LibreOffice's key, and the update-lock templates of
  their user guide wrote org.openoffice.Office.HSTUpdate there. That one subtree, which only HST
  Office has, is still read from the old key unless HST Office's key of the same hive has it; an
  empty subtree there hides the old one (the guide's unlock template writes it). No other setting
  under LibreOffice's key applies to HST Office.
- Every opened policy key is closed on all return paths (the temp-file error paths leaked it).
- shell: the alternative mail sender is read from HKCU\Software\HASON TECH\HST Office\SendAsEMailClient
  instead of LibreOffice's key.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).

